WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:04.417
There is a crisis happening here

00:00:04.417 --> 00:00:08.083
along the border of Belarus and Poland.

00:00:08.083 --> 00:00:12.005
Thousands of migrants trying 
to make it deeper into Europe

00:00:12.005 --> 00:00:17.663
had been trapped in makeshift camps 
in freezing temperatures for weeks.

00:00:17.663 --> 00:00:21.733
Often without water, food,
or medical attention.

00:00:22.775 --> 00:00:24.510
"Thousands of people caught in the middle of

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:26.704
an intensifying geopolitical dispute."

00:00:26.704 --> 00:00:29.378
"Stranded in freezing conditions."

00:00:29.378 --> 00:00:31.560
"Human rights groups say that the harsh conditions

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.799
have already killed some of the migrants."

00:00:34.799 --> 00:00:38.178
Migrants coming to Europe usually 
make their way through countries

00:00:38.178 --> 00:00:40.765
that border the Mediterranean sea.

00:00:40.765 --> 00:00:45.899
But earlier this year, Poland, Lithuania,
and Latvia saw a spike

00:00:45.899 --> 00:00:49.640
in irregular crossings along 
their landlocked borders.

00:00:50.628 --> 00:00:52.350
When you take a closer look

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:59.585
you'll notice these crossings are taking place
along Belarus' entire border with the EU.

00:00:59.585 --> 00:01:04.470
And the reason this border is the new epicenter
of Europe’s migration crisis

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.006
is one man.

00:01:07.006 --> 00:01:08.705
Europe’s last dictator.

00:01:20.568 --> 00:01:26.610
In 2020, Alexander Lukashenko, claimed victory
in Belarus’ presidential election.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:29.396
An election widely believed to be rigged.

00:01:35.354 --> 00:01:37.420
Backed by his main ally Russia,

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:42.722
Lukashenko ruled for four prior terms 
that were also considered fraudulent.

00:01:42.722 --> 00:01:46.859
But this time the response was different.

00:01:46.859 --> 00:01:50.735
After the results were announced, thousands
of Belarusians took to the streets,

00:01:50.735 --> 00:01:55.783
despite the pandemic, to demand 
Lukashenko step down.

00:01:55.783 --> 00:02:02.513
Instead, he responded with a brutal crackdown
and arrested more than 7,000 protesters.

00:02:02.513 --> 00:02:05.255
Hundreds were beaten and tortured.

00:02:05.255 --> 00:02:07.371
Four people died.

00:02:07.371 --> 00:02:12.178
And his political opposition 
was forced to flee the country.

00:02:12.178 --> 00:02:19.580
Lukashenko’s nickname, “Europe’s last dictator” 
became more fitting than ever.

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:21.932
The EU responded with sanctions,

00:02:21.932 --> 00:02:27.410
marking the beginning of a dangerous struggle
between the EU and Belarus.

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:36.027
“The EU will impose sanctions on a substantial
number of individuals responsible for violence,

00:02:36.027 --> 00:02:39.624
repression, and election fraud."

00:02:39.624 --> 00:02:41.870
Despite this first round of sanctions

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:45.000
Lukashenko continued to crush dissent.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:50.000
He used prisons across the country
to hold hundreds of political prisoners.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.590
And arrested 27,000 civilians.

00:02:54.449 --> 00:02:59.730
So, the EU doubled down and imposed 
two more rounds of sanctions.

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:04.280
But Lukashenko only got more aggressive.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:08.340
In May of 2021, Belarus intercepted 
a passenger plane

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:11.695
as it flew over Belarus’ airspace.

00:03:11.695 --> 00:03:15.476
After forcing it to land, 
Belarus officials arrested

00:03:15.476 --> 00:03:18.680
a prominent Belarusian opposition journalist.

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:23.087
He was allegedly forced to confess 
to plotting riots on camera.

00:03:23.087 --> 00:03:26.950
As of today, he is still under house arrest.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:30.626
The EU countered with yet another 
round of sanctions.

00:03:30.626 --> 00:03:35.000
This time, Lukashenko manufactured 
a crisis at the border

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.075
to get back at them.

00:03:39.268 --> 00:03:44.467
Migrants hoping to get to Europe often make
their way to these coastal areas

00:03:44.467 --> 00:03:48.410
and then take a dangerous boat ride 
to European shores.

00:03:49.086 --> 00:03:52.749
It’s an arduous journey that can take days.

00:03:52.749 --> 00:03:55.000
Many don’t make it.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:01.810
But in the summer of 2021, a seemingly easier
path opened up.

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:06.121
Shortly after the fourth round of EU sanctions,
Lukashenko announced:

00:04:06.121 --> 00:04:10.236
He wouldn't be stopping migrants 
from heading to the EU.

00:04:10.236 --> 00:04:12.725
It was more than just a warning.

00:04:12.725 --> 00:04:17.727
Belarus hadn’t seen a lot of Middle Eastern
migrants come through here in the past.

00:04:17.727 --> 00:04:24.435
So the Lukashenko regime created new ways
to lure them into the country.

00:04:24.435 --> 00:04:28.890
Belarus began offering tourist visas to thousands
in the Middle East.

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:32.050
Especially in Iraq.

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:34.520
Flights from Istanbul, Damascus and Dubai

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:37.910
on Belavia, Belarus’ state-owned airline,

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:42.247
more than doubled compared to 
the same time last year.

00:04:42.247 --> 00:04:45.000
Encouraged by Belarus' open borders,

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:49.637
thousands of migrants from war-torn zones
arrived in Minsk.

00:04:49.637 --> 00:04:52.610
Once on the ground, reports show 
Belarusian guards

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:57.440
bussed them to the border along 
Latvia, Lithuania, and Poland.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:01.114
But the EU border wasn’t open to them.

00:05:01.114 --> 00:05:07.628
All three countries declared a state of emergency
at the border and put up barbed wire fences.

00:05:07.628 --> 00:05:12.340
Looking for an opening, thousands of migrants
eventually ended up here,

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:14.831
at this checkpoint on the border

00:05:14.831 --> 00:05:17.136
where things escalated. 

00:05:18.446 --> 00:05:23.589
Poland rushed thousands of troops to the border
to push the migrants back.

00:05:23.589 --> 00:05:29.717
They used tear gas and water canons 
to stop migrants from crossing.

00:05:29.717 --> 00:05:33.974
On the Belarusian side, border guards 
wouldn’t let the migrants retreat.

00:05:33.974 --> 00:05:38.302
They forcibly held them and 
pushed them to try again.

00:05:38.302 --> 00:05:42.951
As many as 4,000 migrants got trapped 
in this violent cycle.

00:05:42.951 --> 00:05:48.380
They couldn't go back to their home countries
and couldn't go to Minsk either.

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:52.900
Those tourist visas were no longer 
available to them.

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:55.166
The EU labeled this “hybrid warfare”.

00:05:55.166 --> 00:05:59.193
And in many ways, it is an act of aggression.

00:05:59.193 --> 00:06:01.520
Belarus trapped the migrants.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:07.400
But the EU also failed the migrants 
by ignoring its own legal code

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:11.360
which prohibits collective expulsions, 
or pushbacks

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:14.660
and guarantees “minimum rights [for] persons

00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:20.361
apprehended or intercepted in connection with
irregular border crossings.”

00:06:20.361 --> 00:06:26.060
Contrary to the law, the migrants here 
were denied their rights and pushed away.

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:32.771
And it’s not the first time it’s happened.

00:06:32.771 --> 00:06:38.838
In 2015 Syria, Iraq, and other Middle Eastern
countries were deep in conflict.

00:06:38.838 --> 00:06:42.391
Forcing many to flee for safety.

00:06:42.391 --> 00:06:47.064
Back then, the safest route into Europe 
went through Turkey.

00:06:47.064 --> 00:06:49.770
From there, migrants could 
cross into Greece by land

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:52.766
or take short boat rides across.

00:06:52.766 --> 00:06:56.400
Hundreds of thousands of migrants 
came in this way.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:00.620
It was the largest migration wave 
ever recorded in a single year

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:04.564
and the EU wasn’t prepared.

00:07:04.564 --> 00:07:09.750
So in March of 2016, the EU 
made a deal with Turkey.

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:15.705
Put simply, Turkey would hold back migrants
and take back any who made it to Greece.

00:07:15.705 --> 00:07:20.985
In exchange, the EU would pay Turkey 
€6 billion.

00:07:20.985 --> 00:07:25.942
With this deal the EU moved its external borders
from here to here.

00:07:25.942 --> 00:07:31.623
Essentially, outsourcing its border security
and legal responsibilities.

00:07:31.623 --> 00:07:35.574
Human rights groups warned 
the deal was “reckless” and “illegal”

00:07:35.574 --> 00:07:39.523
and made migrants “subject to 
human rights violations.”

00:07:39.523 --> 00:07:44.840
Still, the deal went on to take effect and
migrant arrivals in Greece plummeted.

00:07:45.736 --> 00:07:50.155
The deal was so effective in stopping 
the flow of migrants

00:07:50.155 --> 00:07:54.841
it soon took on a new geopolitical importance.

00:07:54.841 --> 00:07:59.050
Over the years, Turkey learned it could threaten
to let migrants in

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:02.600
in exchange for more funds from the EU.

00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:06.276
But it’s not just Turkey.

00:08:06.276 --> 00:08:11.140
Europe has paid all these countries 
to keep migrants from its borders.

00:08:11.944 --> 00:08:18.777
You’ll notice they form a buffer around Europe 
that stretches from Turkey to Morocco.

00:08:18.777 --> 00:08:24.299
In many of these places, migrants are subject
to abuse and violence

00:08:24.299 --> 00:08:29.460
including being pushed back to the conflict
areas they are trying to escape.

00:08:30.484 --> 00:08:33.850
But Europe usually gets away 
with pushing back migrants

00:08:33.850 --> 00:08:38.896
because it happens here, far away 
and out of sight.

00:08:38.896 --> 00:08:45.120
And this gives all these countries leverage
and incentive to use migrants as pawns.

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:50.000
And that’s exactly what Lukashenko 
is doingin Belarus

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:53.722
to try to get the EU to drop sanctions.

00:08:53.722 --> 00:08:56.485
But it doesn't seem to be working.

00:09:09.253 --> 00:09:14.279
After weeks of escalation, the EU adopted
yet another round of sanctions

00:09:14.279 --> 00:09:19.780
and pressured airlines to cut off major air routes 
from the Middle East to Minsk.

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:24.846
Belarus has finally admitted to moving migrants
to the border with Poland.

00:09:24.846 --> 00:09:30.529
And has brought the migrants from the cold
camps to nearby warehouses for now.

00:09:30.529 --> 00:09:34.321
But Lukashenko hasn't fully backed down yet.

00:09:34.321 --> 00:09:37.977
In fact, he's still encouraging migrants to
cross into the EU.

00:09:49.895 --> 00:09:54.220
And that’s because as long as the EU continues
to incentivize countries

00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:57.354
to keep migrants away from its borders

00:09:57.354 --> 00:10:01.803
leaders like Lukashenko 
will find ways to exploit them.

